## 2. DATASETS
===========================================================================
CrossCodeEval (Python), primary
  - Task: complete the rest of a partial line.
  - Verified on all 2,665 examples: prompt + groundtruth + right_context gives
    the exact file text (context_start_lineno = 0; newline count in prompt =
    groundtruth_start_lineno; groundtruth is a single line; right_context is
    empty in 7 examples where the target is the last line).
  - 1,368 distinct target files, 471 repos.
  - Repos with >= 3 distinct files: 206 repos, 2,018 examples.
  - Repos with >= 5 distinct files: 79 repos, 1,237 examples.
  - Not verified: content matches the real GitHub files.
  - Repo names end in a 7-char hex hash (likely a pinned commit, inferred).

RepoBench v1.1 (Python), second dataset
  - Task: predict the next line.
  - Fields (from Hugging Face metadata, verify locally): gold_snippet_index,
    context (list of snippets with identifier, path, snippet).
  - No train split. Splits are task settings.
  - data/repobench/ currently has only download.py: download first.
  - Verify: field names, task settings (prefer cross-file settings), candidates
    per example, snippet paths, repos and distinct paths per repo, consistency
    of all_code (or equivalent).

Rules for both
  - Report results per dataset. Do not pool examples across datasets.
  - Common internal format (repo, path, text, target); only loaders and filters
    differ per dataset.
  - Fixed random seed for every split and sampling step.
  - Save split manifests (repo, path, split).

===========================================================================
## 3. SPLIT DESIGN (per repo, by FILE, never by example)
===========================================================================
Keep repos with >= 3 distinct files. Split files into:
  - BASE files (about 50 to 60%): form the knowledge pool. Used for retrieval
    AND for fine-tuning.
  - NEW files (about 15 to 20%): available to retrieval only, never in
    fine-tuning. Used for the staleness test. Only for repos with >= 5 files;
    for smaller repos, skip NEW and put these files in BASE or TEST.
  - TEST files (about 25 to 30%): targets are evaluated. Never in the pool,
    the index, or the fine-tuning data.
At least 2 BASE files and 1 TEST file per repo.

Conditions:
  - MAIN condition: pool = BASE. Systems A, B, C all have access to the same
    information through different channels. Used for the headline comparison.
  - STALENESS condition: add NEW files to the retrieval index only (A and C).
    Evaluate test examples whose relevant files are in NEW. B (and the
    fine-tuned part of C) never saw them. Tests the claim that RAG handles
    changed or new code and fine-tuning does not.
  - Note: with few files per repo, staleness results come from a subset of
    repos (about 79 for CrossCodeEval). Report sample sizes.

Fine-tuning corpus: pooled across repos (one adapter per dataset), built from
BASE files only. Validation set from BASE files, never from TEST.

CrossCodeEval pool construction
  1. file_text = prompt + groundtruth + right_context
  2. Deduplicate by (repo, file path): one copy per key.
  3. Optional enrichment: pre-retrieved cross-file snippets from examples,
     excluding any whose path or content comes from a TEST file. Note in the
     thesis that these were selected by the benchmark authors (mild bias).

RepoBench pool construction
  1. Per-repo pool keyed by file path from context snippets and the code field.
  2. Deduplicate by (repo, path, snippet text).
  3. Pool entries are fragments, not full files (chunk-level fine-tuning).

