## 4. LEAKAGE ASSERTIONS (automate in preprocessing; must pass before use)
===========================================================================
  - No TEST file path appears in the pool, index, or fine-tuning data.
  - No test groundtruth or next_line string appears in the fine-tuning corpus.
  - Snippet filtering checked by both path match and content match.
  - Validation data comes from BASE files only.
  - NEW files absent from the fine-tuning corpus (for the staleness test).
  - RepoBench: a test example's target file may appear as snippet source in
    other examples, so filter by path, not only by example.

===========================================================================
## 5. SYSTEMS
===========================================================================
System A (RAG-only)
  - Chunk pool files (256 lines, 32 overlap); snippets as chunks for RepoBench.
  - microsoft/unixcoder-base embeddings, FAISS IndexFlatIP, normalized vectors,
    per-repo index.
  - BM25 baseline retriever (Wang et al. found BM25 beat dense embedders).
  - Top-k sweep (for example 1, 3, 5, 10).
System B (Fine-tuning only)
  - Training examples from BASE files with cut points inside lines
    (prefix to rest of line for CrossCodeEval; prefix to next line for
    RepoBench).
  - QLoRA, 4-bit NF4, adapters on attention projection layers.
  - No retrieval at inference.
System C (Hybrid)
  - Fine-tuned model plus retrieval, same retrieval and prompt format as A.
  - Option: fine-tune with retrieval-formatted prompts (RAFT-style variant).
  - Fix the missing import in the hybrid pipeline first.

===========================================================================
## 6. EVALUATION
===========================================================================
Completion: exact match, edit similarity, identifier accuracy, CodeBLEU.
Retrieval: P@k, R@k, MRR (rule per dataset in A3; report dropped share).
Cost: latency and memory per system; accuracy gain per added latency and per
  added GB.
Extra analyses:
  1. Hit vs miss: accuracy when the relevant file was and was not retrieved.
  2. Noisy-context injection: irrelevant chunks, compare degradation.
  3. Staleness test (NEW files, see Section 3).
  4. Forgetting check: small HumanEval run before and after fine-tuning.
  5. Cross-file-dependent subset reported separately.
  6. Top-k sweep.
Rigor: bootstrap confidence intervals; multiple seeds for fine-tuning if time
  allows.

