## 7. HYPOTHESES (write into the thesis BEFORE running experiments)
===========================================================================
  H1: hybrid > RAG-only > FT-only on cross-file-dependent examples.
  H2: the hybrid's gain shrinks when retrieval quality is high.
  H3: FT-only degrades most on NEW files and unseen code.
  H4 (low-data): with only a few files per repo, fine-tuning adds little
      relative to RAG. A legitimate finding either way.

Expected vs Wang et al. (2025) section
  Record their findings, then your predictions for this setting:
  - RAG with suitable retrieval beats fine-tuning alone.
  - Combination gives the best accuracy.
  - BM25 beat dense embedders (UniXcoder, CodeT5) on C++.
  - Retrieval greatly increases input length and cuts throughput; fine-tuning
    costs upfront training but no runtime latency.
  - Fine-tuning degraded general code generation (forgetting).
  - Their setting: one company's C++, about 120,000 files, 0.5B to 7B models.
  - Yours: public Python benchmarks, much smaller per-repo data, 1.5B with
    QLoRA. State where you expect to match and where you expect to differ.

